#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157010 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157229 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb27ab3a-0ca0-5e64-94ce-24a393d86f64" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Truth Table"}] :question/id #uuid "1901808f-9cd7-52c2-85a7-8937aa8d4da3" :question/math-academy-id "e-13222" :question/problem "What are the missing values in the following truth table?\n\n| $\\,X\\,$ | $\\,Y\\,$ | $\\,X \\land (X \\lor \\neg Y) \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{T}}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{T}}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, we add a column for $\\neg Y{:}$\n\n| $\\,X\\,$ | $\\,Y\\,$ | $\\, \\neg Y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n\nThen, we add a column for $X \\lor \\neg Y{:}$\n\n| $\\,X\\,$ | $\\,Y\\,$ | $\\, \\neg Y \\,$ | $\\, X \\lor \\neg Y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n\nFinally, we add a column for $X \\land (X \\lor \\neg Y){:}$\n\n| $\\,X\\,$ | $\\,Y\\,$ | $\\, \\neg Y \\,$ | $\\, X \\lor \\neg Y \\,$ | $\\, X \\land (X \\lor \\neg Y) \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157011 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157230 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a6a36e8-e14e-5051-acae-48de63ff3eff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalent Statements Using Partially Completed Truth Tables"}] :question/id #uuid "beb3d6c7-1c43-5d6b-a817-e1756ea36818" :question/math-academy-id "e-13223" :question/problem "Find the missing values in the following truth table:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,\\neg A \\land \\neg B \\,$ | $\\,\\neg (A \\lor B) \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |\n\nUse your results to determine whether $\\neg A \\land \\neg B$ is logically equivalent to $\\neg (A \\lor B).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's solve for the missing values:\n\n- When $A \\equiv \\textrm T$ and $B \\equiv \\textrm F,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬A∧¬B & ≡¬\\text{T}∧¬\\text{F} \\\\  & ≡\\text{F}∧\\text{T} \\\\  & ≡\\text{F}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- When $A \\equiv \\textrm F$ and $B \\equiv \\textrm T,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(A∨B) & ≡¬(\\text{F}∨\\text{T}) \\\\  & ≡¬\\text{T} \\\\  & ≡\\text{F}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- When $A \\equiv \\textrm F$ and $B \\equiv \\textrm F,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(A∨B) & ≡¬(\\text{F}∨\\text{F}) \\\\  & ≡¬\\text{F} \\\\  & ≡\\text{T}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur completed table is shown below.\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,\\neg A \\land \\neg B \\,$ | $\\,\\neg (A \\lor B) \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n\n \n\nTo determine whether $\\neg A \\land \\neg B$ is logically equivalent to $\\neg (A \\lor B),$ we need to check whether their truth tables are equivalent.\n\nLooking at the last two columns, we see that they are the same $(\\textrm{F},\\textrm{F},\\textrm{F},\\textrm{T}).$ Therefore, we conclude that $\\neg A \\land \\neg B$ is logically equivalent to $\\neg (A \\lor B),$ that is,\n\n$$\n\\neg A \\land \\neg B \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\equiv} \\neg (A \\lor B).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157012 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc5b8688-3838-5e9e-9304-9a4c787034ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Logically Equivalent Statements"}] :question/id #uuid "b6448ed1-56af-57e0-b414-8c01e6a01571" :question/math-academy-id "e-13224" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are logically equivalent?\n\n- $B \\land \\neg A$\n\n- $\\neg (A \\lor \\neg B)$\n\n- $\\neg (\\neg A \\lor B)$" :question/worked-solution "Two statements are logically equivalent if they have the same truth table. So, we construct a truth table for the three given statements:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, B \\land \\neg A \\,$ | $\\, \\neg (A \\lor \\neg B) \\,$ | $\\, \\neg (\\neg A \\lor B) \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n\nWe see that only the statements $B \\land \\neg A$ and $\\neg (A \\lor \\neg B)$ have the same truth table, so they are logically equivalent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571407 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc5b8688-3838-5e9e-9304-9a4c787034ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Logically Equivalent Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d16d097-d7aa-5ffe-bee2-48e3a453468b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570910} {:answer/id #uuid "4147f9fb-3cf6-5c90-9091-5db61b63fab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570988} {:answer/id #uuid "85c9254d-fa16-53ba-8824-4e1914265d1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571099} {:answer/id #uuid "c3928ceb-899c-5b8d-9495-f4c446ac0b05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571193} {:answer/id #uuid "e2884a12-3356-5c32-a805-fd39f121224d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85c9254d-fa16-53ba-8824-4e1914265d1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571099} :answer-field/id #uuid "47faa176-79c4-52dc-b052-4e8bdcb68270" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60ece590-e8a6-5abb-9e47-471816aaf089" :question/math-academy-id "q-127961" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are logically equivalent?\n\nI. $¬(A∨B)$\n\nII. $A∨¬B$\n\nIII. $¬(A∧B)$" :question/worked-solution "Two statements are logically equivalent if they have the same truth table. So, we construct a truth table for the three given statements:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,¬(A∨B)\\,$ | $\\,A∨¬B\\,$ | $\\,¬(A∧B)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n\nWe see that none of the statements have the same truth table, so none of them are logically equivalent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571378 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc5b8688-3838-5e9e-9304-9a4c787034ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Logically Equivalent Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00dfcdf9-ccb2-5f6c-a192-151925743c47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570895} {:answer/id #uuid "1569c631-3e1c-545e-bca8-4b339bbf907d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570921} {:answer/id #uuid "26c6b578-4875-5aa4-8f07-11014a12692d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570949} {:answer/id #uuid "82f488ba-2af6-5a11-9c9c-28b885bd3a16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and 